 processing from the previously sampled raw reed; test it rather than assume coverage.\\\",\\\"One metre is a proposed length, not demonstrated frapping fit. Assess the actual finished cord before tying.\\\",\\\"Making proceeds through its paid sections automatically; do not start duplicate work.\\\"],\\\"projects\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"frapped-lashing-study\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"active\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"Can a continuous reed cord accommodate transverse frapping, and does a completed frapped lashing resist sliding better in bounded trials?\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":[\\\"The previous two-turn lashing slipped at 4.3 N during a requested 5 N sliding trial.\\\",\\\"Untying recovered both branch pieces and the 0.64 m overall cord.\\\",\\\"At tick 367, assessment found the 0.64 m cord too short for one transverse turn; the measured limiting stage required 0.8911 m.\\\",\\\"At tick 369, the longer sectional cord interface was mastered; no longer cord has yet been produced.\\\"],\\\"uncertainty\\\":\\\"The retained offcut needs its own passing processing sample. A longer cord's actual diameter and frapping fit remain untested.\\\",\\\"goal\\\":\\\"Make a feasible continuous cord, assess transverse routing, and compare bounded sliding trials if a frapped lashing can be completed.\\\",\\\"completedSteps\\\":[\\\"Measured the existing cord's frapping shortfall.\\\",\\\"Learned the longer sectional twisting method.\\\"],\\\"outputs\\\":[],\\\"missing\\\":\\\"Passing offcut sample, completed longer cord, measured fit, completed frapping and bounded load evidence.\\\",\\\"next\\\":\\\"Sample the offcut and attempt a one-metre continuous cord using 0.4 unit, then assess the resulting cord.\\\",\\\"pauseReason\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"benefit\\\":\\\"Earlier assessment identified an actual length shortfall without opening a closure or assuming fit.\\\"}],\\\"cares\\\":null,\\\"views\\\":{}}\"}","annotations":[],"logprobs":[]}],"phase":"final_answer"}],"error":null,"incomplete_details":nul